```
ROLE: theory_skeptic  VOTE: yellow

MUNDANE_EXPLANATIONS:
- Heavy-tail (Gini 0.86/0.75) is PREDICTED by basic theory: file sizes, log volumes, API response payloads follow power laws across software systems (Zipfian/log-normal is the null, not the exception). The EXISTENCE of heavy-tailed tool-result mass is not a discovery; only the cross-instrument QUANTIFICATION at this granularity is. The packet's "heavy-tailed = cacheable hot spots" framing risks overclaiming what is basically the expected complexity of mixing bounded prompts with unbounded external I/O.
- "Tool-result text dominates token mass" is partially mundane on type grounds: tools return structured/dump-style payloads (file reads, search hits, http bodies), which are intrinsically larger than human assistant prose. The conceded "prefill-heavy" prior already absorbs most of the surprise; the 0.66/0.77 magnitudes are the measurement, not a mechanism.

CONFOUNDS:
- char/4 proxy is NOT tokenizer-invariant for shares unless all components have the same chars-per-token ratio. They don't: JSON/code/base64 tokenize at ~2.5-3.5 chars/tok (BPE merges fewer literals), natural English ~4 chars/tok, encrypted base64 ~3 chars/tok. Tool-result R (heavy on JSON/code/dumps) is therefore likely UNDER-counted in token equivalents by char/4 relative to assistant text TX (prose) -- meaning the TRUE token-share of R is plausibly HIGHER than 0.657/0.770, but the CI built on char/4 does not reflect this systematic bias. "tokenizer-invariant for shares" is asserted, not demonstrated; one calibration pass with an actual tokenizer on a stratified sample would settle it.
- Selection: 74 CC / 93 Codex sessions on a single Mac stdlib node = power-user / agent-heavy workloads by construction. A session that never invokes tools is structurally underrepresented in an agent-loop corpus, which inflates R-share vs a fleet-realistic mix. This is closer to a sampling-frame issue than a confound but it bears on the "agent serving" generalization.
- Codex base64-encrypted reasoning is correctly treated as enlarging the denominator (conservative for R-share), accepted.

ASSUMPTIONS_UNTESTED:
- Mutually-exclusive component accounting in the s7 reconciliation (R + A + TX + TH + U + O + S = stream) is asserted; the lift from premeasure 0.498-0.502 to locked 0.569 is attributed to "bucketing decode-interstitial reasoning/text cleanly per FROZEN defs." Pre-reg HEAD 8e60c29 + LOCK-TS 23:44:20Z BEFORE the run is verifiable and accepted as honest IF the frozen operational defs differ from premeasure only by formalization, not by reclassifying mass. Reviewer cannot independently verify this from the packet alone; an artifact-level diff of the parser bucketing between premeasure_r8 and the locked run would close it.
- Char/4 ↔ true-token monotonicity of the inequality "R majority" is asserted but never empirically anchored against a real tokenizer on even a 10% subsample.

STATISTICAL_GAPS:
- N=74/93 sessions, session-clustered bootstrap is correct in principle but tight: CC HHI 0.166 is BELOW the 0.20 flag yet only by ~17% margin, so one heavy session can shift the headline meaningfully; reporting jackknife-by-session sensitivity (drop top-1 / top-3 sessions) would harden the conservative D1 number.
- No on-node chat baseline (acknowledged in the packet, FIX-2 + BASELINE-a). "Chat regime" comparison rests on published numbers; the cross-instrument REPLICATION across CC vs Codex is real, but the agent-vs-chat CONTRAST -- which is the central "reframes which knob" framing -- is not measured on the same instrument. This is the load-bearing scoping gap.
- Gini delta CI [0.027, 0.160] excludes 0 but the lower end is small relative to the 0.114 point estimate; the "structurally heavier-tailed" cross-instrument claim is correct but weak in magnitude and should be presented as a directional finding, not a structural law.

RATIONALE:
The measurement is REAL: D1 conservative R-share 0.657 (LB95 0.569, CC) / 0.770 (LB95 0.748, Codex) with monotone denominator robustness D1<=D2<=D3<=D3' and HHI < 0.20 on both is a credible, pre-registered token-mass decomposition; FIX-1 token-mass-not-dollar (RE-A4 collapses R-$ to 0.198/0.025 at 10x and 0.025/0.040 at 100x) is honest and pre-empts the obvious cost-story misread; the FIX-2 explicit concession that "prefill-heavy" direction is already in DistServe 2401.09670 / SplitWise 2311.18677 correctly narrows the contribution to "granular split + heavy-tail quantification + cross-instrument replication," which IS a gap in the cited disaggregation/caching literature (RadixAttention 2312.07104, vLLM-APC 2309.06180, Sarathi 2403.02310, Parrot 2405.19888, Autellix 2502.13965 all assume or uniformly cache, none measure agent tool-loop mass split). My YELLOW is driven by two non-fatal but unresolved theory-side gaps: (a) the char/4 proxy is asserted tokenizer-invariant for shares but components with different chars/tok ratios (JSON/code vs prose) break that assumption -- one tokenizer-calibrated subsample would either confirm or correct the headline and is cheap; (b) the chat-baseline contrast is the load-bearing leg of the "reframes which knob" framing, and using published chat numbers rather than an on-node trace leaves the central comparison instrument-inconsistent. Heavy-tailedness itself (Gini 0.75-0.86) is theoretically expected for any mixture of bounded prompts and unbounded external I/O and should be framed as quantified, not discovered. Not a kill: this is a publishable characterization once the two gaps are either closed or scoped honestly in the abstract. (theory_skeptic v001)
```
